      "content": "<h3>How the nodes are linked</h3>\n<p>Inside, a LinkedList keeps only three fields: <code>first</code> (the first node), <code>last</code> (the last node) and <code>size</code>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\">Node</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">item</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">prev</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">next</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Node 1 (first)</td><td>Raigad</td><td>null</td><td>Node 2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Node 2</td><td>Sinhagad</td><td>Node 1</td><td>Node 3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Node 3 (last)</td><td>Torna</td><td>Node 2</td><td>null</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>To insert a new fort after Node 1, Java creates one new node and re-points two links on each side. Nothing else moves. That is why inserting is cheap <em>once you are at the right place</em>, and finding that place is the expensive part.</p>"
