      "content": "<h3>2. addFirst, push and offerFirst</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>void addFirst(E e);  void push(E e);  boolean offerFirst(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> nothing / nothing / <code>true</code></li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> These put the element at the <strong>front</strong>. Adding at the front is where LinkedList beats ArrayList: no shifting. <code>push</code> is the name used when the list acts like a <strong>stack</strong> (last in, first out).</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> <code>push(e)</code> is just <code>addFirst(e)</code>. Together with <code>pop()</code> (which removes the first element) you get a stack that is consistent with the <code>Deque</code> contract. Note: the legacy <code>Stack</code> class works at the other end and is synchronized.</p>",
